      "math_academy_id": "q-183792",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Describe the set $S=\\{2,1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},...\\}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n$$\nS=\\{{{field-1}}\\,:\\,n\\in {{field-2}}\\}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the elements of our set\n\n$$\n2,\\,1,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,…\n$$\n\nform an infinite geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=\\frac{1}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}}{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $n$th term ${a}_{n}$ of our sequence for $n\\in N,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set using a constructive definition as follows:\n\n$$\nS=\\{4\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$",
